One day, a Western villager carried water across the valley. As she walked, the water mysteriously tripled in her bucket. She offered it all to the Eastern villagers, who used it to grow abundant crops. Grateful, they returned half their harvest.

Both villages prospered, but some Western villagers grew fearful. "What if we give our water and receive nothing back?" they asked. They began keeping their water, and their land remained barren.

Others continued sharing. Each time water was given freely, it multiplied threefold. Each time the Eastern villagers received this gift, they returned generously, knowing their prosperity depended on continued trust.

The elders taught: "The greatest wealth flows not from hoarding what you have, but from trusting others with your gifts. When we send forth our resources in faith, they multiply. When we receive abundance, we must honor that faith by sharing back. Thus, both sides flourish where either alone would perish."
